{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LjXv6U371RKSkXXRxrCa3s","type":"tool_result","content":"S for VSG on G with V S G ( S ) < K . []\n\n\n\n3. The VSG is NP-complete\n\nThe NP-completeness proof of VSG is done by reducing a modified version of\nthe rain-cut linear arrangement problem to it (see [3]). This modification\nminimizes the number of edges passing over each vertex rather than those\npassing between neighboring vertices in the arrangement.\nDefinition 6. An instance of the Modified Mincut Linear Arrangement Problem\n(MMCLA) is a pair (G, K) where G=(V, E) is an undirected graph with vertex\nset V={vl, ..., v,} and K is a non-negative integer, called the with. The\ninstance (G, K) is positive if there is a labeling 2: V--,{1, ..., n} of the vertices\nsuch that\n\n                            max {width(vi) I 1 <=i<=N}<=K\nwhere\n                     width(vi) = I{{vj,, vh}eEI2(vh)<i<2(v~2)}l.\n\nThe M M C L A can be proved NP-complete by reducing S I M P L E M A X C U T\nto it (see [3]) in a similar fashion as done in [2] with the standard rain-cut\nlinear arrangement problem.\nTheorem 7. The VSG is NP-complete.\nProof Let (G=(V,E), K) be an instance of the MMCLA. We construct an\ninstance (G', K') of VSG as follows:\n\f474                                                                                     Th. Lengauer\n\nLet V= {v 1, ..., v,}. Then we define G':=(V', E') where\n\n      V' =--{v/(j)[ l ~i<=N, O<=j~N}\n      E' = {{vi(j), re(k)} [ 1 <=i<=N,O < j < k ~ N } ~ { { v i ( j ) , vj(i)}l{vi, vj}~E}\n      K'=K+N.\nThus each vertex in G becomes an (N + 1)-clique in G'. Edges between vertices\nin G become edges between corresponding cliques in G', such that edges\nconverge in different vertices. A vertex vi(j) in the (N+l)-clique Ci\n ={v/(j)[O<j<N} representing vertex vieV has at most one edge leaving C i.\nFurthermore C/has at least one vertex v~(0) that has no edges leaving C/.\n    We now show that (G, K) is a positive instance of the M M C L A if and only\nif (G', K') is a positive instance of VSG.\n    \"~\":       Assume      that   there   is a     labeling    of G such       that\nmax {width(i)[l<i<N} <K. We will pebble G' achieving a maximum vertex\ncut of size at most K'.\n    We pebble the cliques C~ in the order i = l , ..., N. The vertices in each\nclique are pebbled in the order j = 0, ..., N.\n    It is easy to see that directly after the vertex v/(0) is pebbled the number of\nvertices in the cliques C/+I, ..., C N that are on the cut is exactly width(i). In\nclique C/ vertices v/(1), ..., vi(N ) are on the cut. Those are all vertices on the\ncut. Thus after v/(0) is pebbled the cut has size width(i)+N. From then on\nuntil we finish pebbling Cg the size of the cut does not increase. This is, because\neach vertex v/(1), ..., v/(N) is taken off the cut when it is pebbled. Furthermore\npebbling it can only put vertices outside C/ on the cut. But there is only one\nedge leading outside C/ out of each vertex inside C/. Thus the size of the cut\nnever exceeds\n                      max {width(i)[ 1 <i< N} + N < K + N = K ' .\n\n    \" ~ \" : Conversely assume that we have a strategy for pebbling G' in the\nVSG with a maximum cutsize of at most K' = K + N. We label the vertices of\nG in the sequence in which their corresponding cliques in G' receive their first\npebble.\n    We now show that with this labeling width(vi)+N is at most as large as\nthe cut-size in G directly after the clique C/received its first pebble. In order to\nshow this we prove that at this time an edge that contributes to width(v/) in G\nhas at least one of its end vertices on the cut in G'. Let {vj,, vj~} be an edge\ncontributing to width(v/) in G. In G', {Vjl{jz),I)j2 , (Jl)} is an edge between a\nclique Cj, that has already received its first pebble, and a clique Cj~ that has\nnot yet received a pebble. If vi,(j2) has a pebble, then vj2(jt ) is on the cut, since\nthis vertex, being in Cj~ is still pebble-free. If vj,(jz ) is pebble-free, it itself is on\nthe cut, since some vertex in Cj~ is already pebbled.\n    Thus each edge contributing to width(v/) in G also contributes a vertex to\nthe cut in G'. All these vertices are outside C r Furthermore, since C/ just\nreceived its first pebble, there are N vertices on the cut in C/. Thus the cut has\nsize at least width(v~)+N. Maximizing over i completes the proof. []\n\fBlack-White Pebbles and Graph Separation                                                       475\n\n4. Conclusions\n\nWe have established a link between the progressive black-white pebble game\nPBWP and a game VSG on undirected graphs that makes the concept of a\nvertex separator dynamic. The VSG is a natural game on undirected graphs,\nand is closely related to certain graph layout and searching problems. In fact,\nit is the vertex separator version of an edge separator game that is better\nknown as the min-cut linear arrangement problem of undirected graphs, and is\nof importance to linear circuit layout. By linking PBWP to VSG, we exhibit a\ncorrelation between two important problem domains - the register allocation\nproblem for nondeterministic straight-line programs, and the search for small\n=== MEGIDDO 125-260 ===\nonly guess the sequence in which the edges are cleared, from which it is straight-\nforward to check whether or not that sequence can be achieved using K or fewer\nsearchers. We now show that the problem is in fact NP-complete.\n  THEOREM 1.      The question: Given G and K, can G be cleared with K searchers?\nis NP-complete.\n PROOF. We prove this by providing a transformation from the following known\nNP-complete problem [2]:\nMIN-CUT INTO EQUAL-SIZED SUBSETS\nINSTANCE: Graph G = (V, E) with 1 V( even, positive integer K.\nQUESTION: Is there a partition of Vinto two subsets V, and V2with 1V, 1= I Vz I = 1I VI\n           suchthat l{{u,v)EE:uE        V,,ve vZ)l SK?\n  For the proof, we need several lemmas about searching