ATHWIDTH remains NP-\nhard for distance-hereditary graphs and circle graphs. A graph is distance-hereditary if and only if it can be\nobtained starting from a unique vertex and repeatedely adding a new pendant vertices (with only one new\nneighbour), or vertices which are true twins or false twins of already existing vertices [?]. Recall that two\nvertices x and y are false twins (true twins) if N (x) = N (y) (N (x) ∪ {x} = N (y) ∪ {y}. In particular all\ntrees are distance-hereditary, and by adding true twins we obtain that the graphs obtained from weighted\ntrees by replacing each vertex with a clique module are also distance-hereditary. Therefore Theorem 4\nimplies the NP-hardness of PATHWIDTH when restricted to distance hereditary graphs. Eventually, note\nthat circle graphs contain all distance-hereditary graphs [?].\n=== DIAZ 572-700 ===\n                                                     4. NP-COMPLETENESS RESULTS\n MINPW(G, (X 1 , . . . , X r )) = max |X i | − 1.\n                                  i∈[r]              It is widely believed that showing that\n                                                     a problem is NP-complete is equivalent\n                                                     to prove its computational intractability\nThe pathwidth of G, denoted MINPW(G),                [Garey and Johnson 1979]. The follow-\nis the minimal pathwidth over all possi-             ing theorem indicates the difficulty of the\nble path decompositions of G. The path-              considered layout problems on arbitrary\nwidth problem (PATHWIDTH ) consists in               graphs.\ndetermining a path decomposition with\nminimal pathwidth. Figure 3 shows a                     THEOREM 4.1 The decisional versions\ngraph and one of its path decompositions.            of the layout problems BANDWIDTH, MINLA ,\n\n                                                      ACM Computing Surveys, Vol. 34, No. 3, September 2002.\n\fA Survey of Graph Layout Problems       