(Bodlaender, Gilbert, Hafsteinsson, Kloks [12,13]). If P =\nN P , then for each c ≥ 0, there is no polynomial time algorithm Ac , that given\na graph G and integer k, either correctly outputs that the treewidth of G is more\nthan k, or that the treewidth of G is at most k + c.\nProof. Suppose on the contrary that we have such an algorithm Ac . Take an\ninput (G = (V, E), k) for the NP-complete Treewidth problem. Build a graph\nH by replacing each vertex v ∈ V by a clique with c + 1 vertices, with copies of\nv and w adjacent in H, if and only if v and w are adjacent in G. See Fig. 4. One\ncan show that tw(H) + 1 = (c + 1)(tw(G) + 1). So, if we apply Ac to H with\ntarget value (k + 1)(c + 1) − 1, then one of the following two cases holds. If Ac\ntells that the treewidth of H is at most (k + 1)(c + 1) − 1 + c, then the treewidth\nof H is at most (k + 1)(c + 1) − 1, and the treewidth of G is at most k. If Ac\ntells that the treewidth of H is larger than (k + 1)(c + 1) − 1, then the treewidth\nof G is larger than k. So, we have a polynomial time algorithm that decides on\nthe treewidth of G, contradicting P = N P .                                       \u0007\n                                                                                  \u0006\n    For many years, it was a major open problem whether approximations of\ntreewidth with a constant approximation ratio existed. The ﬁrst breakthrough\nwas obtained by Wu et al. [47].\nTheorem 8 (Wu, Austrin, Pitassi, Liu, 2014 [47]). Suppose the Small Set\nExpansion hypothesis holds. Then, for any α > 1, there is a c > 0, such that there\nis no polynomial time algorithm that for any given graph G with n vertices, either\ncorrectly outputs that pw(G) ≤ c · n, or correctly outputs that tw(G) ≥ α · c · n.\n    The theorem implies that when we assume that the Small Set Expansion\n=== KINNERSLEY ===\n45:ory. Kirousis and Papadimitriou [9] proved that                          The Robertson-Seymour       theorems (see, for\n102:num