{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgiTGn18V11C1enReQGRy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QbJefskRSdfvpER6sVvNwf","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat >> reports/subexp_pathwidth.md <<'EOF'\n\n## 3. The reduction catalogue: every reduction into pathwidth or vertex separation on record, with its blow-up (item 03, 2026-11-04)\n\nThe question of this section is plan §0.4.2: which problems `X` reduce to\npathwidth (equivalently vertex separation, Kinnersley 1992) with a target\ngraph whose vertex count is **linear in the size of the source instance**,\nso that a `2^{o(n)}` algorithm for pathwidth would give `2^{o(N)}` for `X`.\nEvery reduction found in the held papers is listed with its source problem,\nits target, its blow-up and the status of the source after ETH; every one\nclaimed linear is implemented in `learning/subexp_reductions.py` and checked\nagainst brute-force vertex separation on every graph to 7 vertices (§3.5).\nThree of the linear ones are ours, found while checking what the literature\nsupplies; each comes with a proof (§3.4) and was checked before it was\nstated.\n\n### 3.0 What was read, and how\n\n- The held papers, extracted with `pdftotext -layout` to\n  `reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions/` (git-ignored, regenerate\n  per `README.md` there) and read at the reduction: Lengauer 1981 (§3,\n  Theorem 7, pp. 473–474); Megiddo, Hakimi, Garey, Johnson & Papadimitriou\n  1988 (Theorem 1, pp. 20–22); Gustedt 1993 (§4, Theorem 4.1, Lemma 4.2,\n  Corollary 4.3; Definition 2.6); Mihai & Todinca 2009 (§3, Theorems 3–4);\n  Kinnersley 1992 (Theorem 3.1, Corollary 3.2); Linhares & Yanasse 2002\n  (Propositions 1–2, Table 1); Fellows & Langston 1987 (Lemma 4.1); Ohtsuki\n  et al. 1979 (§III); Möhring 1990 (§4, Theorems 4.1–4.4); Díaz, Petit &\n  Serna 2002 (§4, Table III; Lemma 2.4); Bodlaender 1997 (p. 2), 1998;\n  Bodlaender & Kloks 1996 (§1, §7); Kloks, Kratsch & Spinrad 1997 (§1);\n  Bodlaender, Kloks, Kratsch & Müller 1995 (§1); Bodlaender 2024 (Theorem\n  7 and its proof, p. 13); Groenland, Joret, Nadara & Walczak 2021 (§1);\n  Bansal & Katzelnick 2023 (§1); Petit 2011.\n- **Scanned and unreadable** (no text layer, no OCR tool on the machine):\n  Ellis, Sudborough & Turner 1994; Bodlaender, Gilbert, Hafsteinsson & Kloks\n  1991 (WG); Kloks & Bodlaender 1992 (TR); Bodlaender & Möhring 1990; Kloks\n  & Kratsch 1992. For these the catalogue uses what the repository recorded\n  when they were read on paper (`paper1/equivalences.md` §7 for EST Theorem\n  2.2) or what a held secondary source states.\n- **Not held**: Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979 (`literature/MISSING.md` says\n  so: 1979 IEEE ISCAS proceedings, never digitised); Arnborg, Corneil &\n  Proskurowski 1987; Monien & Sudborough 1988; Makedon, Papadimitriou &\n  Sudborough 1985; Möhring 1996; Bonnet 2024/2025; Goldberg, Golumbic,\n  Kaplan & Shamir 1995; Kloks, Bodlaender, Müller & Kratsch 1993; Müller &\n  Wagner 1989; Bodlaender & Thilikos 1995. Each is cited here only as a\n  held paper cites it, and its blow-up is marked unknown.\n- The repository's own chain: `lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/`\n  (loop0005), `MOSPGraph.lean`, `VSEquivPW.lean`, and `paper1/equivalences.md`,\n  which records every Table 1 equivalence with the graph it is stated on.\n\n### 3.1 The catalogue\n\n`N` is the size parameter of the source instance named in the row (vertices\nof the source graph, customers of the MOSP instance, nets of the matrix);\n`n`, `m`, `Δ` are the source graph's vertices, edges and maximum degree. A\nreduction is **linear** when the target has `O(N)` vertices. \"Status after\nETH\" is whether any hypothesis *on record in a held paper* supports a\n`2^{Ω(N)}` lower bound for the source once ETH is voided (plan §0.1).\n\n| # | source problem `X` (size `N`) | target | construction | target vertices | linear? | exact relation | source of the reduction | held? | status of `X` after ETH |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | vertex separation (`n`) | pathwidth | the same graph | `n` | **1** | `vs = pw` | Kinnersley 1992 Thm 3.1 | yes; Lean `VSEquivPW.lean` | the same problem |\n| 2 | gate matrix layout cost, interval thickness, narrowness, one-dimensional logic tracks, monotone/full node search, multiple PLA folding (`N` nets or vertices) | pathwidth | the same graph or matrix (the net graph) | `N` | **1** | each `= pw + 1` | Fellows & Langston 1987 Lemma 4.1; Kinnersley 1992 Cor. 3.2; Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1986; Möhring 1990; Linhares & Yanasse 2002 Table 1 | yes; Lean `Complex/*` (loop0005) | the same problem under another name |\n| 3 | MOSP (`N` customers) | pathwidth of the MOSP graph | customers as vertices, a clique per pattern | `N` | **1** | `mosp = pw + 1` | Yanasse 1997a Prop. 5; Linhares & Yanasse 2002 Prop. 2 | yes; Lean `MOSPGraph.lean` | the same problem (converse, pathwidth → MOSP: `n` customers, `m` patterns, §3.5 row 7) |\n| 4 | edge search number (`n`) | vertex separation | the 2-expansion, every edge subdivided twice | `n + 2m` | linear in `n + m`; in `n` at bounded degree | `es(G) = vs(G'')` | Ellis, Sudborough & Turner 1994 Thm 2.2 | held, scanned; recorded in `equivalences.md` §7, checked there on 181 graphs | `es` is within `+2` of `vs` on the same graph (Lean `Complex/EdgeSearch*.lean`): no independent hardness |\n| 5 | modified min-cut linear arrangement MMCLA (`N` vertices) | vertex separation (VSG) | each vertex an `(N+1)`-clique; edge `v_i v_j` becomes `v_i(j) v_j(i)` | `N (N + 1)` | **quadratic** | `vs(G') = mcw(G) + N` | Lengauer 1981 Thm 7 | yes; checked here to `N = 4` | MMCLA ← Simple Max Cut (Garey & Johnson [3]), blow-up not stated there; no post-ETH support |\n| 6 | minimum cut into equal-sized subsets (bisection; `n`, degree `d`) | edge search number | `n + 1` cliques of size `M = 6 n (n + 2)(d + K)`, `nN` edges between every pair, `+N` to the special clique, `+3` per edge of `G` | `(n + 1) · 6n(n+2)(d+K) = Θ(n³ (d + K))` | **cubic** | `s(H) ≤ s ⟺` bisection `≤ K` | Megiddo et al. 1988 Thm 1, p. 20 | yes | bisection has no `2^{Ω(n)}` support on record after ETH; target is edge search, not `vs` exactly |\n| 7 | vertex separator problem VSP = balanced complete bipartite subgraph of the complement (`n`, `m`; NP-hard on 3-regular graphs, Müller & Wagner 1989) | pathwidth of starlike chordal graphs | central clique `V`, a peripheral clique `{u, w} ∪ n` private vertices per edge | `n + n m` | **quadratic** on 3-regular (`Θ(n²)`), cubic in general | `pw(φ(G)) = n + opt_VSP − 1` (Lemma 4.2) | Gustedt 1993 Thm 4.1 | yes | VSP has no `2^{Ω(n)}` support on record after ETH |\n| 8 | balanced system of 0,1-linear equations (matrix `A`, integers polynomially bounded) | pathwidth of octopus graphs / node-weighted trees | cliques of size given by the integers | the sum of the integers: polynomial, **not linear** in the matrix | pseudo-polynomial | yes/no preserved | Mihai & Todinca 2009 Thms 3–4, from Monien & Sudborough's problem | yes | strongly NP-hard; no `2^{Ω(N)}` support on record |\n| 9 | cutwidth, modified cutwidth on planar graphs of maximum degree 3 (`n`) | vertex separation on planar graphs of maximum degree 3 | not held | unknown | unknown | — | Monien & Sudborough 1988, via Díaz et al. 2002 Table III | no | the cutwidth chain is where Bonnet locates the `√n` loss (§1.2) |\n| 10 | 3-SAT (`N` variables) | treewidth | \"simple, self-contained\" | linear in the formula size by the statement `2^{Ω(n)}` | linear (claimed) | — | Bonnet 2024/2025, abstract | no | ETH: voided if the counting theorem stands; whether the graphs have `pw = tw` is unknown to us |\n| 11 | minimum cut into equal-sized subsets (as the literature recalls it) | treewidth of cobipartite graphs | not held | unknown | unknown | — | Arnborg, Corneil & Proskurowski 1987, via Kloks, Kratsch & Spinrad 1997 §1, BKKM 1995 §1, Möhring 1990 §4, Bodlaender 1997 p. 2 | no | bisection: see row 6 |\n| 12 | interval graph augmentation with minimum clique number (`n`) | pathwidth | the same graph | `n` | **1** | `θ = pw + 1` | Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979 (the original NP-completeness), cited by Ohtsuki et al. 1979 §III and Gustedt 1993 | **missing** | the same problem |\n| 13 | treewidth on cobipartite (or any AT-free) graphs (`n`) | pathwidth on the same class | the same graph | `n` | **1** | `pw = tw` | Möhring 1996; stated in BKKM 1995 Thm 2.2, Bodlaender 1998 Thm 99 | no (secondary held); checked here on every cobipartite graph to 7 vertices and on every double of §3.4 | treewidth on cobipartite graphs: NP-complete (row 11); no `2^{Ω(n)}` support on record after ETH |\n| 14 | pathwidth on all graphs (`n`) | pathwidth = treewidth on **cobipartite** graphs | the **closed-neighbourhood double** `H`: cliques `A = B = V`, `a_u ~ b_v` iff `u = v` or `uv ∈ E` | `2n` | **linear** | `vs(H) = vs(G) + n` | **ours** (§3.4.1); we found no prior statement in the held papers | proved, checked | the same problem: so pathwidth on cobipartite graphs is exactly as hard as in general, at blow-up 2 |\n| 15 | pathwidth on all graphs (`n`), additive gap `c` | pathwidth | the lexicographic product `G[K_c]` | `c n` | **linear** at fixed `c` | `pw(G[K_c]) + 1 = c (pw(G) + 1)` | **ours** for pathwidth (§3.4.2); the treewidth form is Bodlaender, Gilbert, Hafsteinsson & Kloks (Bodlaender 2024 Thm 7) | proved, checked | amplification: `2^{o(n)}` for pathwidth within additive `c − 1` gives `2^{o(n)}` exact |\n| 16 | **cutwidth on graphs of maximum degree 3** (`n`), planar or not | vertex separation on graphs of maximum degree 3, planarity preserved | the **degree-clique expansion** `G°`: a port per (vertex, incident edge), the ports of a vertex a clique, the two ports of an edge joined | `2m + #isolated ≤ 3n` | **linear** | `vs(G°) = cw(G)` when `Δ ≤ 3`; `cw(G) ≤ vs(G°) ≤ cw(G) + ⌊Δ/2⌋ − 1` in general | **ours** (§3.4.3); the natural reading of row 9, which we cannot confirm | proved, checked | cutwidth: `O*(2ⁿ)` is the best algorithm and beating it \"an explicit open problem for a long time\" (Bentert et al. 2025, §1.1); NP-complete on planar max-degree-3 graphs (row 9, not held); no `2^{Ω(n)}` support on record after ETH |\n\nRows 1–3, 12–13 are blow-up 1 (the same object under another name or on a\nsubclass); rows 14–16 are the linear reductions with a genuine\nconstruction; rows 5–8 are the classical NP-completeness reductions, all\nsuperlinear; rows 9–11 are the reductions we could not read.\n\n### 3.2 The best `X` with a linear reduction\n\n**Three candidates, in order of what a transfer theorem would say.**\n\n1. **`X = cutwidth on graphs of maximum degree 3`** (row 16). The\n   reduction `G ↦ G°` is linear (`≤ 3n` vertices), exact (`cw(G) = vs(G°)`\n   for `Δ ≤ 3`), preserves planarity and maximum degree 3, and is proved\n   below. A `2^{o(n)}` pathwidth algorithm — even one for subcubic planar\n   graphs — would give a `2^{o(n)}` algorithm for cutwidth on subcubic\n   planar graphs, the class on which cutwidth is NP-complete (Monien &\n   Sudborough 1988, not held; Díaz et al. 2002 Table III). This is the `X`\n   for item 07: it is the only row whose source is a *different* problem\n   with its own open exponential-time question (Bentert et al. 2025 call\n   beating `O*(2ⁿ)` for cutwidth a long-standing explicit open problem;\n   their §1.1 says the `O*(2ⁿ)` DP remains the best known even for\n   constant-factor approximation). What it does not do: name a surviving\n   *hypothesis*. No held paper supports `2^{Ω(n)}` for cutwidth on subcubic\n   graphs by anything other than ETH, and the chain from 3-SAT to cutwidth\n   on degree-3 graphs is not held and, by Bonnet's account (§1.2), loses a\n   square root somewhere.\n2. **`X = treewidth on cobipartite (hence AT-free, co-comparability)\n   graphs`** (rows 13–14). Blow-up 1 by Möhring's `pw = tw` on AT-free\n   graphs (not held; checked here on every cobipartite graph to 7 vertices\n   and on every double). The converse, row 14, is the finding that matters\n   for item 04: **pathwidth on cobipartite graphs is as hard as pathwidth on\n   all graphs at blow-up 2**, so no class containing the cobipartite graphs\n   (AT-free, co-comparability, the \"`pw = tw`\" classes of\n   `paper2/survey_intersection_classes.md`) can be an easier class for the\n   positive track, and a positive result on cobipartite graphs would be a\n   positive result in general. In the frame of §2:\n   `PathwidthSubexpOn cobipartite → PathwidthSubexp` is a consequence of\n   `vs(H) = vs(G) + n` and the composition `Prop` of §2.1, with `f(2n) =\n   o(n)` from `f = o(n)`.\n3. **`X = MOSP` and the rest of the complex** (rows 1–3, 12): blow-up 1,\n   already in Lean; they say nothing new (the same problem).\n\n**What is not on record.** We found no linear reduction *from a problem\nwith a surviving post-ETH lower bound* into pathwidth: no reduction from\n`k`-SAT, Orthogonal Vectors, Set Cover, Hamiltonian Path or Max Cut into\npathwidth or vertex separation appears in any held paper, and the two that\nthe literature attributes to such sources (Bonnet from 3-SAT into\ntreewidth, row 10; ACP from bisection into cobipartite treewidth, row 11)\nare not held. The bisection chain of §1.2 (3-SAT → Max Cut → bisection →\ncobipartite treewidth = pathwidth) therefore stays a candidate with one\nunverified step, row 11. Item 07's theorem is accordingly a transfer to\ncutwidth on subcubic graphs (and to cobipartite pathwidth), not to a\nhypothesis; plan §0.6's \"item 03 lowers item 07's ambition\" applies in\nthis form: **the transfer theorem exists and is linear, but its `X` carries\nno hypothesis of its own.**\n\n### 3.3 What the superlinear reductions lose, and where\n\n- **Lengauer** (row 5) needs the offset `N` to make every clique's `N`\n  pebble-free vertices dominate the count while one clique is entered,\n  so that an edge contributing to `width(v_i)` contributes a vertex to the\n  cut; the clique size is the vertex count, hence `N(N + 1)`. Checked here\n  to `N = 4` (§3.5).\n- **Megiddo et al.** (row 6) need `M = 6n(n+2)(d+K)` so that the special\n  clique's `M − 1` searchers (Lemma 1) dominate every other term; the\n  `nN` edges between every pair of cliques force the balanced split, and\n  the three edges per edge of `G` carry the cut. Cubic in `n` at best.\n- **Gustedt** (row 7) adds `|V(G)|` private vertices to every peripheral\n  clique so that every normalized path decomposition has the central\n  clique in one bag and the peripheral cliques on two sides; `n · m`.\n- **Mihai & Todinca** (row 8) build cliques whose sizes are the integers of\n  a strongly NP-hard equation system, so the vertex count is the sum of\n  the (polynomially bounded) integers: polynomial, not linear in the matrix.\n\nSo every classical NP-completeness reduction into pathwidth or its\nequivalents is superlinear in the vertices, by construction and not by\naccident: each uses clique size to simulate a weight or an offset. That is\nthe mechanism behind Bonnet's `2^{Ω(√n)}` remark (§1.2): a quadratic step\nin the chain costs exactly a square root in the exponent.\n\n### 3.4 The three linear constructions, with proofs\n\nThroughout, a layout is an ordering `σ` of `V`; `vs_σ(t)` is the number of\nvertices among the first `t` with a neighbour among the rest; `vs(G) = min_σ\nmax_t vs_σ(t)`. Every statement here was checked on every graph to 7\nvertices (§3.5) before it was written.\n\n**3.4.1 The closed-neighbourhood cobipartite double.** For `G = (V, E)`\nwith `|V| = n`, let `H` have vertex set `A ∪ B`, `A = {a_v}`, `B = {b_v}`,\n`A` and `B` cliques, and `a_u b_v ∈ E(H)` iff `u = v` or `uv ∈ E`.\n\n*Theorem.* `vs(H) = vs(G) + n`.\n\n*Proof.* (`≤`) Take `σ` optimal for `G` and lay out `H` as `a_{σ(1)}, …,\na_{σ(n)}, b_{σ(1)}, …, b_{σ(n)}`. A cut inside `A` counts at most `n − 1`.\nAfter all of `A` and the first `t < n` vertices of `B`: the `t` placed\n`b`'s count (they have unplaced clique-mates), and `a_u` counts iff `u` has\nan unplaced closed neighbour, i.e. `u ∈ N_G[S_{>t}]` where `S_{>t}` is the\nsuffix of `σ`; `|N[S_{>t}]| = (n − t) + vs_σ(t)`. So the cut is `n +\nvs_σ(t) ≤ n + vs(G)`.\n(`≥`) Take a layout of `H` of value `k`. One of the two cliques finishes\nfirst; by the symmetry of `H` assume it is `A`, at position `p`, with `j`\nvertices of `B` before `p` (`j < n`). Let `τ` be the order of `V` in which\nthe `b`'s appear. At the cut just before position `p`, `n − 1` vertices of\n`A` and `j` of `B` are placed and every one of them has an unplaced\nclique-mate, so `k ≥ n − 1 + j`. At the cut after the `t`-th `b` for `t ≥\nj` (so after `p`), the `t` placed `b`'s count and `a_u` counts iff `u ∈\nN[S^τ_{>t}]`, so `k ≥ n + vs_τ(t)`. For `t < j`, `vs_τ(t) ≤ t ≤ j − 1 ≤ k −\nn`. Hence `k ≥ n + max_t vs_τ(t) ≥ n + vs(G)`. ∎\n\n`H` is cobipartite, so by Möhring (1996) `tw(H) = pw(H)`; checked on every\ndouble to 7 source vertices (§3.5). The map is computable in `O(n²)` time\nand has `2n` vertices.\n\n**3.4.2 The clique blow-up.** `G[K_c]` replaces every vertex by a `c`-clique\nand every edge by a complete join.\n\n*Theorem.* `vs(G[K_c]) + 1 = c (vs(G) + 1)`.\n\n*Proof.* (`≤`) Lay out the blocks in an optimal order `σ` of `G`, each\nblock consecutively. At a block boundary after `t` blocks the count is\n`c · vs_σ(t)`. Inside block `v` with `i < c` copies placed, the vertices\ncounted are the copies of earlier vertices with a neighbour at or after\n`v`, at most `c · vs_σ(t − 1)` of them (`v` itself is a later neighbour of\nsuch a vertex), plus the `i ≤ c − 1` placed copies of `v`; so at most `c ·\nvs(G) + c − 1`.\n(`≥`) Take a layout of `G[K_c]` of value `k` and let `τ` order `V` by the\nposition of the *last* copy. For `2 ≤ t ≤ n`, at the cut just before the\nlast copy of `v_t`: every `u` earlier in `τ` with a neighbour `w` at or\nafter `v_t` in `τ` has all `c` copies counted (`w` has an unplaced copy,\nadjacent to every copy of `u`), and the `c − 1` placed copies of `v_t`\ncount; the first set contains the `vs_τ(t − 1)` vertices with a neighbour\nafter `v_{t−1}`, so `k ≥ c · vs_τ(t − 1) + c − 1` for every cut of `τ`,\ni.e. `k + 1 ≥ c (vs(G) + 1)`. (`n = 1`: `vs(K_c) = c − 1`.) ∎\n\n**3.4.3 The degree-clique expansion.** `G°` has a port `(v, e)` for every\nvertex `v` and incident edge `e`; the ports of `v` form a clique `K_v`; the\ntwo ports of an edge are joined; isolated vertices are kept. `|V(G°)| = 2m\n+ #isolated`, maximum degree `Δ(G°) ≤ Δ(G)`, and `G°` is planar when `G`\nis (each vertex is blown into a small cycle in the rotation order).\n\n*Theorem.* `cw(G) ≤ vs(G°)` for every graph, and `vs(G°) ≤ cw(G) +\nmax(0, ⌊Δ/2⌋ − 1)`; in particular **`vs(G°) = cw(G)` when `Δ(G) ≤ 3`**.\n\n*Proof.* (`cw ≤ vs(G°)`) Take a layout of `G°` of value `k` and order `V`\nby the position of the last port of each vertex (`τ`). For a cut of `τ`\nafter `v_i`, every crossing edge `uw` (`u` complete before `w`) contributes\na distinct counted port at the `G°`-cut at the last port of `v_i`: if the\nport `(w, uw)` is unplaced, the placed port `(u, uw)` has it as an\nunplaced neighbour; if it is placed, it has an unplaced clique-mate since\n`K_w` is incomplete. So `cw_τ ≤ k`.\n(`vs(G°) ≤ cw + …`) Take a cutwidth layout of `G` of value `k`, lay out the\ncliques in that order, and within `K_v` place first the `a` ports whose\nedges go left, then the `b` ports whose edges go right. Let `c` be the\nedges passing over `v`; `c + a ≤ k` and `c + b ≤ k`. While the left ports\nare placed the count is `c + a` (a left endpoint leaves the cut exactly\nwhen its port enters it); while the `j`-th right port is placed the count\nis `c + a + j` for `j < b` (the left ports still have an unplaced\nclique-mate) and `c + b` at `j = b`. So the maximum is `c + max(a, b) +\nmax(0, min(a, b) − 1) ≤ k + max(0, ⌊Δ/2⌋ − 1)`, and for `a + b ≤ 3` the\nexcess is zero. ∎\n\nThe excess is real: for `K_{1,4}`, `vs(G°) = cw + 1` (the test\n`test_degree_clique_expansion_exceeds_cutwidth_at_degree_four`), and §3.5\nrecords the measured excess by `Δ`. Degree 3 is therefore the boundary of\nthis construction, which is where the literature's hardness for vertex\nseparation sits (planar, maximum degree 3: row 9); we take this as the\nlikely shape of Monien & Sudborough's reduction without being able to\nconfirm it.\n\n### 3.5 The checks\n\n`python -m learning.subexp_reductions` (every graph of the networkx atlas,\n1 to 7 vertices, 1,252 graphs; `nice -n 19`; log\n`reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions/check_atlas7.log`, results\n`check.json`). Two exact evaluators of vertex separation are used — the\nliteral minimum over permutations and a prefix-set DP (`vs_subset_dp`,\nnumpy, to about 20 vertices) — and the DP is checked against the\npermutations on every atlas graph first. MOSP values come from a DP over\n*pattern* subsets that shares no code with the layout evaluators.\n\nCHECK_TABLE_PLACEHOLDER\n\n`tests/test_subexp_reductions.py` (8 tests) runs the same checks to 5\nvertices in the gate.\n\n### 3.6 What this does not claim\n\n- That rows 9–11 are what their papers say: Monien & Sudborough 1988,\n  Arnborg, Corneil & Proskurowski 1987 and Bonnet 2025 are not held; their\n  constructions and blow-ups are \"unknown\", not \"superlinear\". If ACP's\n  construction is linear, the bisection chain of §1.2 is a linear\n  ETH-chain into cobipartite pathwidth, and §3.2 changes only in that the\n  hypothesis at its top is ETH, the one already voided.\n- That `X = cutwidth on subcubic graphs` carries a surviving hypothesis.\n  It carries an open problem (Bentert et al. 2025) and an NP-completeness\n  we have not read.\n- That the three constructions of §3.4 are new: \"we found no prior\n  statement in the held papers\" (the treewidth form of 3.4.2 is on record;\n  3.4.3 is the natural reading of row 9; 3.4.1 may well be how ACP or\n  Möhring's readers argued). Nothing is claimed \"first\".\n- That Möhring's `pw = tw` on AT-free graphs is verified: it is checked on\n  cobipartite graphs to 7 vertices and on the doubles to 14, and cited\n  from secondary sources.\n- That any reduction here is in Lean: that is item 08's work. The\n  statements of §3.4 are stated against layouts exactly as\n  `VertexSeparation.lean` defines them so that item 08 can take them as\n  written.\n- That the checks to 7 vertices prove anything: they precede the proofs, as\n  TASK.md requires, and the proofs are the claim.\n\n### 3.7 Regenerate\n\n    D=reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions\n    # the extracts (git-ignored): see $D/README.md for the full list\n    nice -n 19 python3 -m learning.subexp_reductions --nmax 7 > $D/check_atlas7.log\n    python3 -m pytest tests/test_subexp_reductions.py -q\n    python3 $D/explore.py 6     # the exploration that preceded §3.4 (not the deliverable)\nEOF\ntail -5 reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions/check_atlas7.log; uptime","description":"Draft §3 of the report; check the atlas run's progress"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3713,"cache_read_input_tokens":238489,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3713,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8d527e6a-db35-43d6-976e-698f424f9f86","uuid":"2945c385-88d7-4346-a57b-94dd845bc48f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T19:40:59.257Z","request_id":"req_011CgiTGmbKcey5eMpNHteoP","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QbJefskRSdfvpER6sVvNwf":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && cat >> reports/subexp_pathwidth.md <<'EOF'\n\n## 3. The reduction catalogue: every reduction into pathwidth or vertex separation on record, with its blow-up (item 03, 2026-11-04)\n\nThe question of this section is plan §0.4.2: which problems `X` reduce to\npathwidth (equivalently vertex separation, Kinnersley 1992) with a target\ngraph whose vertex count is **linear in the size of the source instance**,\nso that a `2^{o(n)}` algorithm for pathwidth would give `2^{o(N)}` for `X`.\nEvery reduction found in the held papers is listed with its source problem,\nits target, its blow-up and the status of the source after ETH; every one\nclaimed linear is implemented in `learning/subexp_reductions.py` and checked\nagainst brute-force vertex separation on every graph to 7 vertices (§3.5).\nThree of the linear ones are ours, found while checking what the literature\nsupplies; each comes with a proof (§3.4) and was checked before it was\nstated.\n\n### 3.0 What was read, and how\n\n- The held papers, extracted with `pdftotext -layout` to\n  `reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions/` (git-ignored, regenerate\n  per `README.md` there) and read at the reduction: Lengauer 1981 (§3,\n  Theorem 7, pp. 473–474); Megiddo, Hakimi, Garey, Johnson & Papadimitriou\n  1988 (Theorem 1, pp. 20–22); Gustedt 1993 (§4, Theorem 4.1, Lemma 4.2,\n  Corollary 4.3; Definition 2.6); Mihai & Todinca 2009 (§3, Theorems 3–4);\n  Kinnersley 1992 (Theorem 3.1, Corollary 3.2); Linhares & Yanasse 2002\n  (Propositions 1–2, Table 1); Fellows & Langston 1987 (Lemma 4.1); Ohtsuki\n  et al. 1979 (§III); Möhring 1990 (§4, Theorems 4.1–4.4); Díaz, Petit &\n  Serna 2002 (§4, Table III; Lemma 2.4); Bodlaender 1997 (p. 2), 1998;\n  Bodlaender & Kloks 1996 (§1, §7); Kloks, Kratsch & Spinrad 1997 (§1);\n  Bodlaender, Kloks, Kratsch & Müller 1995 (§1); Bodlaender 2024 (Theorem\n  7 and its proof, p. 13); Groenland, Joret, Nadara & Walczak 2021 (§1);\n  Bansal & Katzelnick 2023 (§1); Petit 2011.\n- **Scanned and unreadable** (no text layer, no OCR tool on the machine):\n  Ellis, Sudborough & Turner 1994; Bodlaender, Gilbert, Hafsteinsson & Kloks\n  1991 (WG); Kloks & Bodlaender 1992 (TR); Bodlaender & Möhring 1990; Kloks\n  & Kratsch 1992. For these the catalogue uses what the repository recorded\n  when they were read on paper (`paper1/equivalences.md` §7 for EST Theorem\n  2.2) or what a held secondary source states.\n- **Not held**: Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979 (`literature/MISSING.md` says\n  so: 1979 IEEE ISCAS proceedings, never digitised); Arnborg, Corneil &\n  Proskurowski 1987; Monien & Sudborough 1988; Makedon, Papadimitriou &\n  Sudborough 1985; Möhring 1996; Bonnet 2024/2025; Goldberg, Golumbic,\n  Kaplan & Shamir 1995; Kloks, Bodlaender, Müller & Kratsch 1993; Müller &\n  Wagner 1989; Bodlaender & Thilikos 1995. Each is cited here only as a\n  held paper cites it, and its blow-up is marked unknown.\n- The repository's own chain: `lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/`\n  (loop0005), `MOSPGraph.lean`, `VSEquivPW.lean`, and `paper1/equivalences.md`,\n  which records every Table 1 equivalence with the graph it is stated on.\n\n### 3.1 The catalogue\n\n`N` is the size parameter of the source instance named in the row (vertices\nof the source graph, customers of the MOSP instance, nets of the matrix);\n`n`, `m`, `Δ` are the source graph's vertices, edges and maximum degree. A\nreduction is **linear** when the target has `O(N)` vertices. \"Status after\nETH\" is whether any hypothesis *on record in a held paper* supports a\n`2^{Ω(N)}` lower bound for the source once ETH is voided (plan §0.1).\n\n| # | source problem `X` (size `N`) | target | construction | target vertices | linear? | exact relation | source of the reduction | held? | status of `X` after ETH |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | vertex separation (`n`) | pathwidth | the same graph | `n` | **1** | `vs = pw` | Kinnersley 1992 Thm 3.1 | yes; Lean `VSEquivPW.lean` | the same problem |\n| 2 | gate matrix layout cost, interval thickness, narrowness, one-dimensional logic tracks, monotone/full node search, multiple PLA folding (`N` nets or vertices) | pathwidth | the same graph or matrix (the net graph) | `N` | **1** | each `= pw + 1` | Fellows & Langston 1987 Lemma 4.1; Kinnersley 1992 Cor. 3.2; Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1986; Möhring 1990; Linhares & Yanasse 2002 Table 1 | yes; Lean `Complex/*` (loop0005) | the same problem under another name |\n| 3 | MOSP (`N` customers) | pathwidth of the MOSP graph | customers as vertices, a clique per pattern | `N` | **1** | `mosp = pw + 1` | Yanasse 1997a Prop. 5; Linhares & Yanasse 2002 Prop. 2 | yes; Lean `MOSPGraph.lean` | the same problem (converse, pathwidth → MOSP: `n` customers, `m` patterns, §3.5 row 7) |\n| 4 | edge search number (`n`) | vertex separation | the 2-expansion, every edge subdivided twice | `n + 2m` | linear in `n + m`; in `n` at bounded degree | `es(G) = vs(G'')` | Ellis, Sudborough & Turner 1994 Thm 2.2 | held, scanned; recorded in `equivalences.md` §7, checked there on 181 graphs | `es` is within `+2` of `vs` on the same graph (Lean `Complex/EdgeSearch*.lean`): no independent hardness |\n| 5 | modified min-cut linear arrangement MMCLA (`N` vertices) | vertex separation (VSG) | each vertex an `(N+1)`-clique; edge `v_i v_j` becomes `v_i(j) v_j(i)` | `N (N + 1)` | **quadratic** | `vs(G') = mcw(G) + N` | Lengauer 1981 Thm 7 | yes; checked here to `N = 4` | MMCLA ← Simple Max Cut (Garey & Johnson [3]), blow-up not stated there; no post-ETH support |\n| 6 | minimum cut into equal-sized subsets (bisection; `n`, degree `d`) | edge search number | `n + 1` cliques of size `M = 6 n (n + 2)(d + K)`, `nN` edges between every pair, `+N` to the special clique, `+3` per edge of `G` | `(n + 1) · 6n(n+2)(d+K) = Θ(n³ (d + K))` | **cubic** | `s(H) ≤ s ⟺` bisection `≤ K` | Megiddo et al. 1988 Thm 1, p. 20 | yes | bisection has no `2^{Ω(n)}` support on record after ETH; target is edge search, not `vs` exactly |\n| 7 | vertex separator problem VSP = balanced complete bipartite subgraph of the complement (`n`, `m`; NP-hard on 3-regular graphs, Müller & Wagner 1989) | pathwidth of starlike chordal graphs | central clique `V`, a peripheral clique `{u, w} ∪ n` private vertices per edge | `n + n m` | **quadratic** on 3-regular (`Θ(n²)`), cubic in general | `pw(φ(G)) = n + opt_VSP − 1` (Lemma 4.2) | Gustedt 1993 Thm 4.1 | yes | VSP has no `2^{Ω(n)}` support on record after ETH |\n| 8 | balanced system of 0,1-linear equations (matrix `A`, integers polynomially bounded) | pathwidth of octopus graphs / node-weighted trees | cliques of size given by the integers | the sum of the integers: polynomial, **not linear** in the matrix | pseudo-polynomial | yes/no preserved | Mihai & Todinca 2009 Thms 3–4, from Monien & Sudborough's problem | yes | strongly NP-hard; no `2^{Ω(N)}` support on record |\n| 9 | cutwidth, modified cutwidth on planar graphs of maximum degree 3 (`n`) | vertex separation on planar graphs of maximum degree 3 | not held | unknown | unknown | — | Monien & Sudborough 1988, via Díaz et al. 2002 Table III | no | the cutwidth chain is where Bonnet locates the `√n` loss (§1.2) |\n| 10 | 3-SAT (`N` variables) | treewidth | \"simple, self-contained\" | linear in the formula size by the statement `2^{Ω(n)}` | linear (claimed) | — | Bonnet 2024/2025, abstract | no | ETH: voided if the counting theorem stands; whether the graphs have `pw = tw` is unknown to us |\n| 11 | minimum cut into equal-sized subsets (as the literature recalls it) | treewidth of cobipartite graphs | not held | unknown | unknown | — | Arnborg, Corneil & Proskurowski 1987, via Kloks, Kratsch & Spinrad 1997 §1, BKKM 1995 §1, Möhring 1990 §4, Bodlaender 1997 p. 2 | no | bisection: see row 6 |\n| 12 | interval graph augmentation with minimum clique number (`n`) | pathwidth | the same graph | `n` | **1** | `θ = pw + 1` | Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979 (the original NP-completeness), cited by Ohtsuki et al. 1979 §III and Gustedt 1993 | **missing** | the same problem |\n| 13 | treewidth on cobipartite (or any AT-free) graphs (`n`) | pathwidth on the same class | the same graph | `n` | **1** | `pw = tw` | Möhring 1996; stated in BKKM 1995 Thm 2.2, Bodlaender 1998 Thm 99 | no (secondary held); checked here on every cobipartite graph to 7 vertices and on every double of §3.4 | treewidth on cobipartite graphs: NP-complete (row 11); no `2^{Ω(n)}` support on record after ETH |\n| 14 | pathwidth on all graphs (`n`) | pathwidth = treewidth on **cobipartite** graphs | the **closed-neighbourhood double** `H`: cliques `A = B = V`, `a_u ~ b_v` iff `u = v` or `uv ∈ E` | `2n` | **linear** | `vs(H) = vs(G) + n` | **ours** (§3.4.1); we found no prior statement in the held papers | proved, checked | the same problem: so pathwidth on cobipartite graphs is exactly as hard as in general, at blow-up 2 |\n| 15 | pathwidth on all graphs (`n`), additive gap `c` | pathwidth | the lexicographic product `G[K_c]` | `c n` | **linear** at fixed `c` | `pw(G[K_c]) + 1 = c (pw(G) + 1)` | **ours** for pathwidth (§3.4.2); the treewidth form is Bodlaender, Gilbert, Hafsteinsson & Kloks (Bodlaender 2024 Thm 7) | proved, checked | amplification: `2^{o(n)}` for pathwidth within additive `c − 1` gives `2^{o(n)}` exact |\n| 16 | **cutwidth on graphs of maximum degree 3** (`n`), planar or not | vertex separation on graphs of maximum degree 3, planarity preserved | the **degree-clique expansion** `G°`: a port per (vertex, incident edge), the ports of a vertex a clique, the two ports of an edge joined | `2m + #isolated ≤ 3n` | **linear** | `vs(G°) = cw(G)` when `Δ ≤ 3`; `cw(G) ≤ vs(G°) ≤ cw(G) + ⌊Δ/2⌋ − 1` in general | **ours** (§3.4.3); the natural reading of row 9, which we cannot confirm | proved, checked | cutwidth: `O*(2ⁿ)` is the best algorithm and beating it \"an explicit open problem for a long time\" (Bentert et al. 2025, §1.1); NP-complete on planar max-degree-3 graphs (row 9, not held); no `2^{Ω(n)}` support on record after ETH |\n\nRows 1–3, 12–13 are blow-up 1 (the same object under another name or on a\nsubclass); rows 14–16 are the linear reductions with a genuine\nconstruction; rows 5–8 are the classical NP-completeness reductions, all\nsuperlinear; rows 9–11 are the reductions we could not read.\n\n### 3.2 The best `X` with a linear reduction\n\n**Three candidates, in order of what a transfer theorem would say.**\n\n1. **`X = cutwidth on graphs of maximum degree 3`** (row 16). The\n   reduction `G ↦ G°` is linear (`≤ 3n` vertices), exact (`cw(G) = vs(G°)`\n   for `Δ ≤ 3`), preserves planarity and maximum degree 3, and is proved\n   below. A `2^{o(n)}` pathwidth algorithm — even one for subcubic planar\n   graphs — would give a `2^{o(n)}` algorithm for cutwidth on subcubic\n   planar graphs, the class on which cutwidth is NP-complete (Monien &\n   Sudborough 1988, not held; Díaz et al. 2002 Table III). This is the `X`\n   for item 07: it is the only row whose source is a *different* problem\n   with its own open exponential-time question (Bentert et al. 2025 call\n   beating `O*(2ⁿ)` for cutwidth a long-standing explicit open problem;\n   their §1.1 says the `O*(2ⁿ)` DP remains the best known even for\n   constant-factor approximation). What it does not do: name a surviving\n   *hypothesis*. No held paper supports `2^{Ω(n)}` for cutwidth on subcubic\n   graphs by anything other than ETH, and the chain from 3-SAT to cutwidth\n   on degree-3 graphs is not held and, by Bonnet's account (§1.2), loses a\n   square root somewhere.\n2. **`X = treewidth on cobipartite (hence AT-free, co-comparability)\n   graphs`** (rows 13–14). Blow-up 1 by Möhring's `pw = tw` on AT-free\n   graphs (not held; checked here on every cobipartite graph to 7 vertices\n   and on every double). The converse, row 14, is the finding that matters\n   for item 04: **pathwidth on cobipartite graphs is as hard as pathwidth on\n   all graphs at blow-up 2**, so no class containing the cobipartite graphs\n   (AT-free, co-comparability, the \"`pw = tw`\" classes of\n   `paper2/survey_intersection_classes.md`) can be an easier class for the\n   positive track, and a positive result on cobipartite graphs would be a\n   positive result in general. In the frame of §2:\n   `PathwidthSubexpOn cobipartite → PathwidthSubexp` is a consequence of\n   `vs(H) = vs(G) + n` and the composition `Prop` of §2.1, with `f(2n) =\n   o(n)` from `f = o(n)`.\n3. **`X = MOSP` and the rest of the complex** (rows 1–3, 12): blow-up 1,\n   already in Lean; they say nothing new (the same problem).\n\n**What is not on record.** We found no linear reduction *from a problem\nwith a surviving post-ETH lower bound* into pathwidth: no reduction from\n`k`-SAT, Orthogonal Vectors, Set Cover, Hamiltonian Path or Max Cut into\npathwidth or vertex separation appears in any held paper, and the two that\nthe literature attributes to such sources (Bonnet from 3-SAT into\ntreewidth, row 10; ACP from bisection into cobipartite treewidth, row 11)\nare not held. The bisection chain of §1.2 (3-SAT → Max Cut → bisection →\ncobipartite treewidth = pathwidth) therefore stays a candidate with one\nunverified step, row 11. Item 07's theorem is accordingly a transfer to\ncutwidth on subcubic graphs (and to cobipartite pathwidth), not to a\nhypothesis; plan §0.6's \"item 03 lowers item 07's ambition\" applies in\nthis form: **the transfer theorem exists and is linear, but its `X` carries\nno hypothesis of its own.**\n\n### 3.3 What the superlinear reductions lose, and where\n\n- **Lengauer** (row 5) needs the offset `N` to make every clique's `N`\n  pebble-free vertices dominate the count while one clique is entered,\n  so that an edge contributing to `width(v_i)` contributes a vertex to the\n  cut; the clique size is the vertex count, hence `N(N + 1)`. Checked here\n  to `N = 4` (§3.5).\n- **Megiddo et al.** (row 6) need `M = 6n(n+2)(d+K)` so that the special\n  clique's `M − 1` searchers (Lemma 1) dominate every other term; the\n  `nN` edges between every pair of cliques force the balanced split, and\n  the three edges per edge of `G` carry the cut. Cubic in `n` at best.\n- **Gustedt** (row 7) adds `|V(G)|` private vertices to every peripheral\n  clique so that every normalized path decomposition has the central\n  clique in one bag and the peripheral cliques on two sides; `n · m`.\n- **Mihai & Todinca** (row 8) build cliques whose sizes are the integers of\n  a strongly NP-hard equation system, so the vertex count is the sum of\n  the (polynomially bounded) integers: polynomial, not linear in the matrix.\n\nSo every classical NP-completeness reduction into pathwidth or its\nequivalents is superlinear in the vertices, by construction and not by\naccident: each uses clique size to simulate a weight or an offset. That is\nthe mechanism behind Bonnet's `2^{Ω(√n)}` remark (§1.2): a quadratic step\nin the chain costs exactly a square root in the exponent.\n\n### 3.4 The three linear constructions, with proofs\n\nThroughout, a layout is an ordering `σ` of `V`; `vs_σ(t)` is the number of\nvertices among the first `t` with a neighbour among the rest; `vs(G) = min_σ\nmax_t vs_σ(t)`. Every statement here was checked on every graph to 7\nvertices (§3.5) before it was written.\n\n**3.4.1 The closed-neighbourhood cobipartite double.** For `G = (V, E)`\nwith `|V| = n`, let `H` have vertex set `A ∪ B`, `A = {a_v}`, `B = {b_v}`,\n`A` and `B` cliques, and `a_u b_v ∈ E(H)` iff `u = v` or `uv ∈ E`.\n\n*Theorem.* `vs(H) = vs(G) + n`.\n\n*Proof.* (`≤`) Take `σ` optimal for `G` and lay out `H` as `a_{σ(1)}, …,\na_{σ(n)}, b_{σ(1)}, …, b_{σ(n)}`. A cut inside `A` counts at most `n − 1`.\nAfter all of `A` and the first `t < n` vertices of `B`: the `t` placed\n`b`'s count (they have unplaced clique-mates), and `a_u` counts iff `u` has\nan unplaced closed neighbour, i.e. `u ∈ N_G[S_{>t}]` where `S_{>t}` is the\nsuffix of `σ`; `|N[S_{>t}]| = (n − t) + vs_σ(t)`. So the cut is `n +\nvs_σ(t) ≤ n + vs(G)`.\n(`≥`) Take a layout of `H` of value `k`. One of the two cliques finishes\nfirst; by the symmetry of `H` assume it is `A`, at position `p`, with `j`\nvertices of `B` before `p` (`j < n`). Let `τ` be the order of `V` in which\nthe `b`'s appear. At the cut just before position `p`, `n − 1` vertices of\n`A` and `j` of `B` are placed and every one of them has an unplaced\nclique-mate, so `k ≥ n − 1 + j`. At the cut after the `t`-th `b` for `t ≥\nj` (so after `p`), the `t` placed `b`'s count and `a_u` counts iff `u ∈\nN[S^τ_{>t}]`, so `k ≥ n + vs_τ(t)`. For `t < j`, `vs_τ(t) ≤ t ≤ j − 1 ≤ k −\nn`. Hence `k ≥ n + max_t vs_τ(t) ≥ n + vs(G)`. ∎\n\n`H` is cobipartite, so by Möhring (1996) `tw(H) = pw(H)`; checked on every\ndouble to 7 source vertices (§3.5). The map is computable in `O(n²)` time\nand has `2n` vertices.\n\n**3.4.2 The clique blow-up.** `G[K_c]` replaces every vertex by a `c`-clique\nand every edge by a complete join.\n\n*Theorem.* `vs(G[K_c]) + 1 = c (vs(G) + 1)`.\n\n*Proof.* (`≤`) Lay out the blocks in an optimal order `σ` of `G`, each\nblock consecutively. At a block boundary after `t` blocks the count is\n`c · vs_σ(t)`. Inside block `v` with `i < c` copies placed, the vertices\ncounted are the copies of earlier vertices with a neighbour at or after\n`v`, at most `c · vs_σ(t − 1)` of them (`v` itself is a later neighbour of\nsuch a vertex), plus the `i ≤ c − 1` placed copies of `v`; so at most `c ·\nvs(G) + c − 1`.\n(`≥`) Take a layout of `G[K_c]` of value `k` and let `τ` order `V` by the\nposition of the *last* copy. For `2 ≤ t ≤ n`, at the cut just before the\nlast copy of `v_t`: every `u` earlier in `τ` with a neighbour `w` at or\nafter `v_t` in `τ` has all `c` copies counted (`w` has an unplaced copy,\nadjacent to every copy of `u`), and the `c − 1` placed copies of `v_t`\ncount; the first set contains the `vs_τ(t − 1)` vertices with a neighbour\nafter `v_{t−1}`, so `k ≥ c · vs_τ(t − 1) + c − 1` for every cut of `τ`,\ni.e. `k + 1 ≥ c (vs(G) + 1)`. (`n = 1`: `vs(K_c) = c − 1`.) ∎\n\n**3.4.3 The degree-clique expansion.** `G°` has a port `(v, e)` for every\nvertex `v` and incident edge `e`; the ports of `v` form a clique `K_v`; the\ntwo ports of an edge are joined; isolated vertices are kept. `|V(G°)| = 2m\n+ #isolated`, maximum degree `Δ(G°) ≤ Δ(G)`, and `G°` is planar when `G`\nis (each vertex is blown into a small cycle in the rotation order).\n\n*Theorem.* `cw(G) ≤ vs(G°)` for every graph, and `vs(G°) ≤ cw(G) +\nmax(0, ⌊Δ/2⌋ − 1)`; in particular **`vs(G°) = cw(G)` when `Δ(G) ≤ 3`**.\n\n*Proof.* (`cw ≤ vs(G°)`) Take a layout of `G°` of value `k` and order `V`\nby the position of the last port of each vertex (`τ`). For a cut of `τ`\nafter `v_i`, every crossing edge `uw` (`u` complete before `w`) contributes\na distinct counted port at the `G°`-cut at the last port of `v_i`: if the\nport `(w, uw)` is unplaced, the placed port `(u, uw)` has it as an\nunplaced neighbour; if it is placed, it has an unplaced clique-mate since\n`K_w` is incomplete. So `cw_τ ≤ k`.\n(`vs(G°) ≤ cw + …`) Take a cutwidth layout of `G` of value `k`, lay out the\ncliques in that order, and within `K_v` place first the `a` ports whose\nedges go left, then the `b` ports whose edges go right. Let `c` be the\nedges passing over `v`; `c + a ≤ k` and `c + b ≤ k`. While the left ports\nare placed the count is `c + a` (a left endpoint leaves the cut exactly\nwhen its port enters it); while the `j`-th right port is placed the count\nis `c + a + j` for `j < b` (the left ports still have an unplaced\nclique-mate) and `c + b` at `j = b`. So the maximum is `c + max(a, b) +\nmax(0, min(a, b) − 1) ≤ k + max(0, ⌊Δ/2⌋ − 1)`, and for `a + b ≤ 3` the\nexcess is zero. ∎\n\nThe excess is real: for `K_{1,4}`, `vs(G°) = cw + 1` (the test\n`test_degree_clique_expansion_exceeds_cutwidth_at_degree_four`), and §3.5\nrecords the measured excess by `Δ`. Degree 3 is therefore the boundary of\nthis construction, which is where the literature's hardness for vertex\nseparation sits (planar, maximum degree 3: row 9); we take this as the\nlikely shape of Monien & Sudborough's reduction without being able to\nconfirm it.\n\n### 3.5 The checks\n\n`python -m learning.subexp_reductions` (every graph of the networkx atlas,\n1 to 7 vertices, 1,252 graphs; `nice -n 19`; log\n`reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions/check_atlas7.log`, results\n`check.json`). Two exact evaluators of vertex separation are used — the\nliteral minimum over permutations and a prefix-set DP (`vs_subset_dp`,\nnumpy, to about 20 vertices) — and the DP is checked against the\npermutations on every atlas graph first. MOSP values come from a DP over\n*pattern* subsets that shares no code with the layout evaluators.\n\nCHECK_TABLE_PLACEHOLDER\n\n`tests/test_subexp_reductions.py` (8 tests) runs the same checks to 5\nvertices in the gate.\n\n### 3.6 What this does not claim\n\n- That rows 9–11 are what their papers say: Monien & Sudborough 1988,\n  Arnborg, Corneil & Proskurowski 1987 and Bonnet 2025 are not held; their\n  constructions and blow-ups are \"unknown\", not \"superlinear\". If ACP's\n  construction is linear, the bisection chain of §1.2 is a linear\n  ETH-chain into cobipartite pathwidth, and §3.2 changes only in that the\n  hypothesis at its top is ETH, the one already voided.\n- That `X = cutwidth on subcubic graphs` carries a surviving hypothesis.\n  It carries an open problem (Bentert et al. 2025) and an NP-completeness\n  we have not read.\n- That the three constructions of §3.4 are new: \"we found no prior\n  statement in the held papers\" (the treewidth form of 3.4.2 is on record;\n  3.4.3 is the natural reading of row 9; 3.4.1 may well be how ACP or\n  Möhring's readers argued). Nothing is claimed \"first\".\n- That Möhring's `pw = tw` on AT-free graphs is verified: it is checked on\n  cobipartite graphs to 7 vertices and on the doubles to 14, and cited\n  from secondary sources.\n- That any reduction here is in Lean: that is item 08's work. The\n  statements of §3.4 are stated against layouts exactly as\n  `VertexSeparation.lean` defines them so that item 08 can take them as\n  written.\n- That the checks to 7 vertices prove anything: they precede the proofs, as\n  TASK.md requires, and the proofs are the claim.\n\n### 3.7 Regenerate\n\n    D=reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions\n    # the extracts (git-ignored): see $D/README.md for the full list\n    nice -n 19 python3 -m learning.subexp_reductions --nmax 7 > $D/check_atlas7.log\n    python3 -m pytest tests/test_subexp_reductions.py -q\n    python3 $D/explore.py 6     # the exploration that preceded §3.4 (not the deliverable)\nEOF\ntail -5 reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions/check_atlas7.log; uptime","description":"Draft §3 of the report; check the atlas run's progress"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01QbJefskRSdfvpER6sVvNwf":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QbJefskRSdfvpER6sVvNwf","type":"tool_result","content":"lengauer                     checked     18  failures 0\nmosp_instance_to_graph       checked   4095  failures 0\nmosp pattern DP vs permutations: 0 failures of 300\nexpansion excess vs(G°) − cw(G) by (Δ, excess): {'4:0': 118, '4:1': 193, '5:0': 42, '5:1': 72, '6:0': 2, '6:1': 10, '6:2': 6}\n38.6 s; written reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions/check.json\n 19:40:59 up 12:07,  1 user,  load average: 31.19, 21.80, 16.69","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8d527e6a-db35-43d6-976e-698f424f9f86","uuid":"f6097c8e-0b9d-4049-a0c8-7ac86efb0971","timestamp":"2026-11-04T19:40:59.360Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"lengauer                     checked     18  failures 0\nmosp_instance_to_graph       checked   4095  failures 0\nmosp pattern DP vs permutations: 0 failures of 300\nexpansion excess vs(G°) − cw(G) by (Δ, excess): {'4:0': 118, '4:1': 193, '5:0': 42, '5:1': 72, '6:0': 2, '6:1': 10, '6:2': 6}\n38.6 s; written reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions/check.json\n 19:40:59 up 12:07,  1 user,  load average: 31.19, 21.80, 16.69","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/home/al/dev/MOSP/reports/subexp_pathwidth.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":664,"oldLines":3,"newStart":664,"newLines":296,"lines":["     lake env lean ../reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/02_frame/axiom_check.lean","     cd .. && cat reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/02_frame/README.md   # the two diffs","     python3 Ralph_Loops/loop0014/gate.py","+","+## 3. The reduction catalogue: every reduction into pathwidth or vertex separation on record, with its blow-up (item 03, 2026-11-04)","+","+The question of this section is plan §0.4.2: which problems `X` reduce to","+pathwidth (equivalently vertex separation, Kinnersley 1992) with a target","+graph whose vertex count is **linear in the size of the source instance**,","+so that a `2^{o(n)}` algorithm for pathwidth would give `2^{o(N)}` for `X`.","+Every reduction found in the held papers is listed with its source problem,","+its target, its blow-up and the status of the source after ETH; every one","+claimed linear is implemented in `learning/subexp_reductions.py` and checked","+against brute-force vertex separation on every graph to 7 vertices (§3.5).","+Three of the linear ones are ours, found while checking what the literature","+supplies; each comes with a proof (§3.4) and was checked before it was","+stated.","+","+### 3.0 What was read, and how","+","+- The held papers, extracted with `pdftotext -layout` to","+  `reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions/` (git-ignored, regenerate","+  per `README.md` there) and read at the reduction: Lengauer 1981 (§3,","+  Theorem 7, pp. 473–474); Megiddo, Hakimi, Garey, Johnson & Papadimitriou","+  1988 (Theorem 1, pp. 20–22); Gustedt 1993 (§4, Theorem 4.1, Lemma 4.2,","+  Corollary 4.3; Definition 2.6); Mihai & Todinca 2009 (§3, Theorems 3–4);","+  Kinnersley 1992 (Theorem 3.1, Corollary 3.2); Linhares & Yanasse 2002","+  (Propositions 1–2, Table 1); Fellows & Langston 1987 (Lemma 4.1); Ohtsuki","+  et al. 1979 (§III); Möhring 1990 (§4, Theorems 4.1–4.4); Díaz, Petit &","+  Serna 2002 (§4, Table III; Lemma 2.4); Bodlaender 1997 (p. 2), 1998;","+  Bodlaender & Kloks 1996 (§1, §7); Kloks, Kratsch & Spinrad 1997 (§1);","+  Bodlaender, Kloks, Kratsch & Müller 1995 (§1); Bodlaender 2024 (Theorem","+  7 and its proof, p. 13); Groenland, Joret, Nadara & Walczak 2021 (§1);","+  Bansal & Katzelnick 2023 (§1); Petit 2011.","+- **Scanned and unreadable** (no text layer, no OCR tool on the machine):","+  Ellis, Sudborough & Turner 1994; Bodlaender, Gilbert, Hafsteinsson & Kloks","+  1991 (WG); Kloks & Bodlaender 1992 (TR); Bodlaender & Möhring 1990; Kloks","+  & Kratsch 1992. For these the catalogue uses what the repository recorded","+  when they were read on paper (`paper1/equivalences.md` §7 for EST Theorem","+  2.2) or what a held secondary source states.","+- **Not held**: Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979 (`literature/MISSING.md` says","+  so: 1979 IEEE ISCAS proceedings, never digitised); Arnborg, Corneil &","+  Proskurowski 1987; Monien & Sudborough 1988; Makedon, Papadimitriou &","+  Sudborough 1985; Möhring 1996; Bonnet 2024/2025; Goldberg, Golumbic,","+  Kaplan & Shamir 1995; Kloks, Bodlaender, Müller & Kratsch 1993; Müller &","+  Wagner 1989; Bodlaender & Thilikos 1995. Each is cited here only as a","+  held paper cites it, and its blow-up is marked unknown.","+- The repository's own chain: `lean/MOSPFormalization/Complex/`","+  (loop0005), `MOSPGraph.lean`, `VSEquivPW.lean`, and `paper1/equivalences.md`,","+  which records every Table 1 equivalence with the graph it is stated on.","+","+### 3.1 The catalogue","+","+`N` is the size parameter of the source instance named in the row (vertices","+of the source graph, customers of the MOSP instance, nets of the matrix);","+`n`, `m`, `Δ` are the source graph's vertices, edges and maximum degree. A","+reduction is **linear** when the target has `O(N)` vertices. \"Status after","+ETH\" is whether any hypothesis *on record in a held paper* supports a","+`2^{Ω(N)}` lower bound for the source once ETH is voided (plan §0.1).","+","+| # | source problem `X` (size `N`) | target | construction | target vertices | linear? | exact relation | source of the reduction | held? | status of `X` after ETH |","+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|","+| 1 | vertex separation (`n`) | pathwidth | the same graph | `n` | **1** | `vs = pw` | Kinnersley 1992 Thm 3.1 | yes; Lean `VSEquivPW.lean` | the same problem |","+| 2 | gate matrix layout cost, interval thickness, narrowness, one-dimensional logic tracks, monotone/full node search, multiple PLA folding (`N` nets or vertices) | pathwidth | the same graph or matrix (the net graph) | `N` | **1** | each `= pw + 1` | Fellows & Langston 1987 Lemma 4.1; Kinnersley 1992 Cor. 3.2; Kirousis & Papadimitriou 1986; Möhring 1990; Linhares & Yanasse 2002 Table 1 | yes; Lean `Complex/*` (loop0005) | the same problem under another name |","+| 3 | MOSP (`N` customers) | pathwidth of the MOSP graph | customers as vertices, a clique per pattern | `N` | **1** | `mosp = pw + 1` | Yanasse 1997a Prop. 5; Linhares & Yanasse 2002 Prop. 2 | yes; Lean `MOSPGraph.lean` | the same problem (converse, pathwidth → MOSP: `n` customers, `m` patterns, §3.5 row 7) |","+| 4 | edge search number (`n`) | vertex separation | the 2-expansion, every edge subdivided twice | `n + 2m` | linear in `n + m`; in `n` at bounded degree | `es(G) = vs(G'')` | Ellis, Sudborough & Turner 1994 Thm 2.2 | held, scanned; recorded in `equivalences.md` §7, checked there on 181 graphs | `es` is within `+2` of `vs` on the same graph (Lean `Complex/EdgeSearch*.lean`): no independent hardness |","+| 5 | modified min-cut linear arrangement MMCLA (`N` vertices) | vertex separation (VSG) | each vertex an `(N+1)`-clique; edge `v_i v_j` becomes `v_i(j) v_j(i)` | `N (N + 1)` | **quadratic** | `vs(G') = mcw(G) + N` | Lengauer 1981 Thm 7 | yes; checked here to `N = 4` | MMCLA ← Simple Max Cut (Garey & Johnson [3]), blow-up not stated there; no post-ETH support |","+| 6 | minimum cut into equal-sized subsets (bisection; `n`, degree `d`) | edge search number | `n + 1` cliques of size `M = 6 n (n + 2)(d + K)`, `nN` edges between every pair, `+N` to the special clique, `+3` per edge of `G` | `(n + 1) · 6n(n+2)(d+K) = Θ(n³ (d + K))` | **cubic** | `s(H) ≤ s ⟺` bisection `≤ K` | Megiddo et al. 1988 Thm 1, p. 20 | yes | bisection has no `2^{Ω(n)}` support on record after ETH; target is edge search, not `vs` exactly |","+| 7 | vertex separator problem VSP = balanced complete bipartite subgraph of the complement (`n`, `m`; NP-hard on 3-regular graphs, Müller & Wagner 1989) | pathwidth of starlike chordal graphs | central clique `V`, a peripheral clique `{u, w} ∪ n` private vertices per edge | `n + n m` | **quadratic** on 3-regular (`Θ(n²)`), cubic in general | `pw(φ(G)) = n + opt_VSP − 1` (Lemma 4.2) | Gustedt 1993 Thm 4.1 | yes | VSP has no `2^{Ω(n)}` support on record after ETH |","+| 8 | balanced system of 0,1-linear equations (matrix `A`, integers polynomially bounded) | pathwidth of octopus graphs / node-weighted trees | cliques of size given by the integers | the sum of the integers: polynomial, **not linear** in the matrix | pseudo-polynomial | yes/no preserved | Mihai & Todinca 2009 Thms 3–4, from Monien & Sudborough's problem | yes | strongly NP-hard; no `2^{Ω(N)}` support on record |","+| 9 | cutwidth, modified cutwidth on planar graphs of maximum degree 3 (`n`) | vertex separation on planar graphs of maximum degree 3 | not held | unknown | unknown | — | Monien & Sudborough 1988, via Díaz et al. 2002 Table III | no | the cutwidth chain is where Bonnet locates the `√n` loss (§1.2) |","+| 10 | 3-SAT (`N` variables) | treewidth | \"simple, self-contained\" | linear in the formula size by the statement `2^{Ω(n)}` | linear (claimed) | — | Bonnet 2024/2025, abstract | no | ETH: voided if the counting theorem stands; whether the graphs have `pw = tw` is unknown to us |","+| 11 | minimum cut into equal-sized subsets (as the literature recalls it) | treewidth of cobipartite graphs | not held | unknown | unknown | — | Arnborg, Corneil & Proskurowski 1987, via Kloks, Kratsch & Spinrad 1997 §1, BKKM 1995 §1, Möhring 1990 §4, Bodlaender 1997 p. 2 | no | bisection: see row 6 |","+| 12 | interval graph augmentation with minimum clique number (`n`) | pathwidth | the same graph | `n` | **1** | `θ = pw + 1` | Kashiwabara & Fujisawa 1979 (the original NP-completeness), cited by Ohtsuki et al. 1979 §III and Gustedt 1993 | **missing** | the same problem |","+| 13 | treewidth on cobipartite (or any AT-free) graphs (`n`) | pathwidth on the same class | the same graph | `n` | **1** | `pw = tw` | Möhring 1996; stated in BKKM 1995 Thm 2.2, Bodlaender 1998 Thm 99 | no (secondary held); checked here on every cobipartite graph to 7 vertices and on every double of §3.4 | treewidth on cobipartite graphs: NP-complete (row 11); no `2^{Ω(n)}` support on record after ETH |","+| 14 | pathwidth on all graphs (`n`) | pathwidth = treewidth on **cobipartite** graphs | the **closed-neighbourhood double** `H`: cliques `A = B = V`, `a_u ~ b_v` iff `u = v` or `uv ∈ E` | `2n` | **linear** | `vs(H) = vs(G) + n` | **ours** (§3.4.1); we found no prior statement in the held papers | proved, checked | the same problem: so pathwidth on cobipartite graphs is exactly as hard as in general, at blow-up 2 |","+| 15 | pathwidth on all graphs (`n`), additive gap `c` | pathwidth | the lexicographic product `G[K_c]` | `c n` | **linear** at fixed `c` | `pw(G[K_c]) + 1 = c (pw(G) + 1)` | **ours** for pathwidth (§3.4.2); the treewidth form is Bodlaender, Gilbert, Hafsteinsson & Kloks (Bodlaender 2024 Thm 7) | proved, checked | amplification: `2^{o(n)}` for pathwidth within additive `c − 1` gives `2^{o(n)}` exact |","+| 16 | **cutwidth on graphs of maximum degree 3** (`n`), planar or not | vertex separation on graphs of maximum degree 3, planarity preserved | the **degree-clique expansion** `G°`: a port per (vertex, incident edge), the ports of a vertex a clique, the two ports of an edge joined | `2m + #isolated ≤ 3n` | **linear** | `vs(G°) = cw(G)` when `Δ ≤ 3`; `cw(G) ≤ vs(G°) ≤ cw(G) + ⌊Δ/2⌋ − 1` in general | **ours** (§3.4.3); the natural reading of row 9, which we cannot confirm | proved, checked | cutwidth: `O*(2ⁿ)` is the best algorithm and beating it \"an explicit open problem for a long time\" (Bentert et al. 2025, §1.1); NP-complete on planar max-degree-3 graphs (row 9, not held); no `2^{Ω(n)}` support on record after ETH |","+","+Rows 1–3, 12–13 are blow-up 1 (the same object under another name or on a","+subclass); rows 14–16 are the linear reductions with a genuine","+construction; rows 5–8 are the classical NP-completeness reductions, all","+superlinear; rows 9–11 are the reductions we could not read.","+","+### 3.2 The best `X` with a linear reduction","+","+**Three candidates, in order of what a transfer theorem would say.**","+","+1. **`X = cutwidth on graphs of maximum degree 3`** (row 16). The","+   reduction `G ↦ G°` is linear (`≤ 3n` vertices), exact (`cw(G) = vs(G°)`","+   for `Δ ≤ 3`), preserves planarity and maximum degree 3, and is proved","+   below. A `2^{o(n)}` pathwidth algorithm — even one for subcubic planar","+   graphs — would give a `2^{o(n)}` algorithm for cutwidth on subcubic","+   planar graphs, the class on which cutwidth is NP-complete (Monien &","+   Sudborough 1988, not held; Díaz et al. 2002 Table III). This is the `X`","+   for item 07: it is the only row whose source is a *different* problem","+   with its own open exponential-time question (Bentert et al. 2025 call","+   beating `O*(2ⁿ)` for cutwidth a long-standing explicit open problem;","+   their §1.1 says the `O*(2ⁿ)` DP remains the best known even for","+   constant-factor approximation). What it does not do: name a surviving","+   *hypothesis*. No held paper supports `2^{Ω(n)}` for cutwidth on subcubic","+   graphs by anything other than ETH, and the chain from 3-SAT to cutwidth","+   on degree-3 graphs is not held and, by Bonnet's account (§1.2), loses a","+   square root somewhere.","+2. **`X = treewidth on cobipartite (hence AT-free, co-comparability)","+   graphs`** (rows 13–14). Blow-up 1 by Möhring's `pw = tw` on AT-free","+   graphs (not held; checked here on every cobipartite graph to 7 vertices","+   and on every double). The converse, row 14, is the finding that matters","+   for item 04: **pathwidth on cobipartite graphs is as hard as pathwidth on","+   all graphs at blow-up 2**, so no class containing the cobipartite graphs","+   (AT-free, co-comparability, the \"`pw = tw`\" classes of","+   `paper2/survey_intersection_classes.md`) can be an easier class for the","+   positive track, and a positive result on cobipartite graphs would be a","+   positive result in general. In the frame of §2:","+   `PathwidthSubexpOn cobipartite → PathwidthSubexp` is a consequence of","+   `vs(H) = vs(G) + n` and the composition `Prop` of §2.1, with `f(2n) =","+   o(n)` from `f = o(n)`.","+3. **`X = MOSP` and the rest of the complex** (rows 1–3, 12): blow-up 1,","+   already in Lean; they say nothing new (the same problem).","+","+**What is not on record.** We found no linear reduction *from a problem","+with a surviving post-ETH lower bound* into pathwidth: no reduction from","+`k`-SAT, Orthogonal Vectors, Set Cover, Hamiltonian Path or Max Cut into","+pathwidth or vertex separation appears in any held paper, and the two that","+the literature attributes to such sources (Bonnet from 3-SAT into","+treewidth, row 10; ACP from bisection into cobipartite treewidth, row 11)","+are not held. The bisection chain of §1.2 (3-SAT → Max Cut → bisection →","+cobipartite treewidth = pathwidth) therefore stays a candidate with one","+unverified step, row 11. Item 07's theorem is accordingly a transfer to","+cutwidth on subcubic graphs (and to cobipartite pathwidth), not to a","+hypothesis; plan §0.6's \"item 03 lowers item 07's ambition\" applies in","+this form: **the transfer theorem exists and is linear, but its `X` carries","+no hypothesis of its own.**","+","+### 3.3 What the superlinear reductions lose, and where","+","+- **Lengauer** (row 5) needs the offset `N` to make every clique's `N`","+  pebble-free vertices dominate the count while one clique is entered,","+  so that an edge contributing to `width(v_i)` contributes a vertex to the","+  cut; the clique size is the vertex count, hence `N(N + 1)`. Checked here","+  to `N = 4` (§3.5).","+- **Megiddo et al.** (row 6) need `M = 6n(n+2)(d+K)` so that the special","+  clique's `M − 1` searchers (Lemma 1) dominate every other term; the","+  `nN` edges between every pair of cliques force the balanced split, and","+  the three edges per edge of `G` carry the cut. Cubic in `n` at best.","+- **Gustedt** (row 7) adds `|V(G)|` private vertices to every peripheral","+  clique so that every normalized path decomposition has the central","+  clique in one bag and the peripheral cliques on two sides; `n · m`.","+- **Mihai & Todinca** (row 8) build cliques whose sizes are the integers of","+  a strongly NP-hard equation system, so the vertex count is the sum of","+  the (polynomially bounded) integers: polynomial, not linear in the matrix.","+","+So every classical NP-completeness reduction into pathwidth or its","+equivalents is superlinear in the vertices, by construction and not by","+accident: each uses clique size to simulate a weight or an offset. That is","+the mechanism behind Bonnet's `2^{Ω(√n)}` remark (§1.2): a quadratic step","+in the chain costs exactly a square root in the exponent.","+","+### 3.4 The three linear constructions, with proofs","+","+Throughout, a layout is an ordering `σ` of `V`; `vs_σ(t)` is the number of","+vertices among the first `t` with a neighbour among the rest; `vs(G) = min_σ","+max_t vs_σ(t)`. Every statement here was checked on every graph to 7","+vertices (§3.5) before it was written.","+","+**3.4.1 The closed-neighbourhood cobipartite double.** For `G = (V, E)`","+with `|V| = n`, let `H` have vertex set `A ∪ B`, `A = {a_v}`, `B = {b_v}`,","+`A` and `B` cliques, and `a_u b_v ∈ E(H)` iff `u = v` or `uv ∈ E`.","+","+*Theorem.* `vs(H) = vs(G) + n`.","+","+*Proof.* (`≤`) Take `σ` optimal for `G` and lay out `H` as `a_{σ(1)}, …,","+a_{σ(n)}, b_{σ(1)}, …, b_{σ(n)}`. A cut inside `A` counts at most `n − 1`.","+After all of `A` and the first `t < n` vertices of `B`: the `t` placed","+`b`'s count (they have unplaced clique-mates), and `a_u` counts iff `u` has","+an unplaced closed neighbour, i.e. `u ∈ N_G[S_{>t}]` where `S_{>t}` is the","+suffix of `σ`; `|N[S_{>t}]| = (n − t) + vs_σ(t)`. So the cut is `n +","+vs_σ(t) ≤ n + vs(G)`.","+(`≥`) Take a layout of `H` of value `k`. One of the two cliques finishes","+first; by the symmetry of `H` assume it is `A`, at position `p`, with `j`","+vertices of `B` before `p` (`j < n`). Let `τ` be the order of `V` in which","+the `b`'s appear. At the cut just before position `p`, `n − 1` vertices of","+`A` and `j` of `B` are placed and every one of them has an unplaced","+clique-mate, so `k ≥ n − 1 + j`. At the cut after the `t`-th `b` for `t ≥","+j` (so after `p`), the `t` placed `b`'s count and `a_u` counts iff `u ∈","+N[S^τ_{>t}]`, so `k ≥ n + vs_τ(t)`. For `t < j`, `vs_τ(t) ≤ t ≤ j − 1 ≤ k −","+n`. Hence `k ≥ n + max_t vs_τ(t) ≥ n + vs(G)`. ∎","+","+`H` is cobipartite, so by Möhring (1996) `tw(H) = pw(H)`; checked on every","+double to 7 source vertices (§3.5). The map is computable in `O(n²)` time","+and has `2n` vertices.","+","+**3.4.2 The clique blow-up.** `G[K_c]` replaces every vertex by a `c`-clique","+and every edge by a complete join.","+","+*Theorem.* `vs(G[K_c]) + 1 = c (vs(G) + 1)`.","+","+*Proof.* (`≤`) Lay out the blocks in an optimal order `σ` of `G`, each","+block consecutively. At a block boundary after `t` blocks the count is","+`c · vs_σ(t)`. Inside block `v` with `i < c` copies placed, the vertices","+counted are the copies of earlier vertices with a neighbour at or after","+`v`, at most `c · vs_σ(t − 1)` of them (`v` itself is a later neighbour of","+such a vertex), plus the `i ≤ c − 1` placed copies of `v`; so at most `c ·","+vs(G) + c − 1`.","+(`≥`) Take a layout of `G[K_c]` of value `k` and let `τ` order `V` by the","+position of the *last* copy. For `2 ≤ t ≤ n`, at the cut just before the","+last copy of `v_t`: every `u` earlier in `τ` with a neighbour `w` at or","+after `v_t` in `τ` has all `c` copies counted (`w` has an unplaced copy,","+adjacent to every copy of `u`), and the `c − 1` placed copies of `v_t`","+count; the first set contains the `vs_τ(t − 1)` vertices with a neighbour","+after `v_{t−1}`, so `k ≥ c · vs_τ(t − 1) + c − 1` for every cut of `τ`,","+i.e. `k + 1 ≥ c (vs(G) + 1)`. (`n = 1`: `vs(K_c) = c − 1`.) ∎","+","+**3.4.3 The degree-clique expansion.** `G°` has a port `(v, e)` for every","+vertex `v` and incident edge `e`; the ports of `v` form a clique `K_v`; the","+two ports of an edge are joined; isolated vertices are kept. `|V(G°)| = 2m","++ #isolated`, maximum degree `Δ(G°) ≤ Δ(G)`, and `G°` is planar when `G`","+is (each vertex is blown into a small cycle in the rotation order).","+","+*Theorem.* `cw(G) ≤ vs(G°)` for every graph, and `vs(G°) ≤ cw(G) +","+max(0, ⌊Δ/2⌋ − 1)`; in particular **`vs(G°) = cw(G)` when `Δ(G) ≤ 3`**.","+","+*Proof.* (`cw ≤ vs(G°)`) Take a layout of `G°` of value `k` and order `V`","+by the position of the last port of each vertex (`τ`). For a cut of `τ`","+after `v_i`, every crossing edge `uw` (`u` complete before `w`) contributes","+a distinct counted port at the `G°`-cut at the last port of `v_i`: if the","+port `(w, uw)` is unplaced, the placed port `(u, uw)` has it as an","+unplaced neighbour; if it is placed, it has an unplaced clique-mate since","+`K_w` is incomplete. So `cw_τ ≤ k`.","+(`vs(G°) ≤ cw + …`) Take a cutwidth layout of `G` of value `k`, lay out the","+cliques in that order, and within `K_v` place first the `a` ports whose","+edges go left, then the `b` ports whose edges go right. Let `c` be the","+edges passing over `v`; `c + a ≤ k` and `c + b ≤ k`. While the left ports","+are placed the count is `c + a` (a left endpoint leaves the cut exactly","+when its port enters it); while the `j`-th right port is placed the count","+is `c + a + j` for `j < b` (the left ports still have an unplaced","+clique-mate) and `c + b` at `j = b`. So the maximum is `c + max(a, b) +","+max(0, min(a, b) − 1) ≤ k + max(0, ⌊Δ/2⌋ − 1)`, and for `a + b ≤ 3` the","+excess is zero. ∎","+","+The excess is real: for `K_{1,4}`, `vs(G°) = cw + 1` (the test","+`test_degree_clique_expansion_exceeds_cutwidth_at_degree_four`), and §3.5","+records the measured excess by `Δ`. Degree 3 is therefore the boundary of","+this construction, which is where the literature's hardness for vertex","+separation sits (planar, maximum degree 3: row 9); we take this as the","+likely shape of Monien & Sudborough's reduction without being able to","+confirm it.","+","+### 3.5 The checks","+","+`python -m learning.subexp_reductions` (every graph of the networkx atlas,","+1 to 7 vertices, 1,252 graphs; `nice -n 19`; log","+`reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions/check_atlas7.log`, results","+`check.json`). Two exact evaluators of vertex separation are used — the","+literal minimum over permutations and a prefix-set DP (`vs_subset_dp`,","+numpy, to about 20 vertices) — and the DP is checked against the","+permutations on every atlas graph first. MOSP values come from a DP over","+*pattern* subsets that shares no code with the layout evaluators.","+","+CHECK_TABLE_PLACEHOLDER","+","+`tests/test_subexp_reductions.py` (8 tests) runs the same checks to 5","+vertices in the gate.","+","+### 3.6 What this does not claim","+","+- That rows 9–11 are what their papers say: Monien & Sudborough 1988,","+  Arnborg, Corneil & Proskurowski 1987 and Bonnet 2025 are not held; their","+  constructions and blow-ups are \"unknown\", not \"superlinear\". If ACP's","+  construction is linear, the bisection chain of §1.2 is a linear","+  ETH-chain into cobipartite pathwidth, and §3.2 changes only in that the","+  hypothesis at its top is ETH, the one already voided.","+- That `X = cutwidth on subcubic graphs` carries a surviving hypothesis.","+  It carries an open problem (Bentert et al. 2025) and an NP-completeness","+  we have not read.","+- That the three constructions of §3.4 are new: \"we found no prior","+  statement in the held papers\" (the treewidth form of 3.4.2 is on record;","+  3.4.3 is the natural reading of row 9; 3.4.1 may well be how ACP or","+  Möhring's readers argued). Nothing is claimed \"first\".","+- That Möhring's `pw = tw` on AT-free graphs is verified: it is checked on","+  cobipartite graphs to 7 vertices and on the doubles to 14, and cited","+  from secondary sources.","+- That any reduction here is in Lean: that is item 08's work. The","+  statements of §3.4 are stated against layouts exactly as","+  `VertexSeparation.lean` defines them so that item 08 can take them as","+  written.","+- That the checks to 7 vertices prove anything: they precede the proofs, as","+  TASK.md requires, and the proofs are the claim.","+","+### 3.7 Regenerate","+","+    D=reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/03_reductions","+    # the extracts (git-ignored): see $D/README.md for the full list","+    nice -n 19 python3 -m learning.subexp_reductions --nmax 7 > $D/check_atlas7.log","+    python3 -m pytest tests/test_subexp_reductions.py -q","+    python3 $D/explore.py 6     # the exploration that preceded §3.4 (not the deliverable)"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/home/al/dev/MOSP/reports/subexp_pathwidth.md"]}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QbJefskRSdfvpER6sVvNwf","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
