{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hdc2dBwtRrnvMqi9tiBzFB","type":"tool_result","content":"1593247       42:32  1.2 claude -p You are iteration 3 of a Ralph loop in the repository at /home/al/dev/MOSP. You have a hard wall-clock cap of 4 hours for this session; plan the work so that the report section and PROGRESS.md are written well before that. If the full study would not fit, sample and say so in the report.  Read these two files first, in full:  --- TASK.md --- # TASK: Pathwidth after ETH — a subexponential exact algorithm for pathwidth, or what its absence would cost  ## Philosophy - **Read first, in full**:   - `reports/subexp_pathwidth_plan.md`, the plan this loop executes. §0.1     is the premise (OpenAI's family 378, *The Exponential Time Hypothesis     is False*, a formally verified subexponential `#k`-SAT algorithm whose     runtime is subexponential in the **number of variables**), §0.2 the     question (does pathwidth have a `2^{o(n)}` exact algorithm, conditional     on that theorem or not; if not, which surviving hypothesis would one     refute through a reduction **linear in the vertices**), §0.3 what is on     record, §0.4 the design decisions, §0.5 the items, §0.6 the kill     criteria, §0.7 the risks. Do not re-decide what §0.4 decides; if an     item finds §0.4 wrong, say so in PROGRESS.md and the report and do the     smallest sound thing.   - `reports/checkpoint_relaxation.md` §10 (the state lattice `F_k`, its     forward and backward frontiers, and why the search sits near the     lattice's floor) and `learning/two_ended.py` (the frontier     enumerator, item 06's instrument).   - `CLAUDE.md`: the owner's rules on compute (only unsolved problems; this     loop runs **no refutation of any certified value or open class**, and     item 06's only runs are frontier counts on generated graphs at     `n ≤ 60`, seconds each), the Lean development's state (no `sorry`, no     `axiom`; `Sandwich.lean`, `VertexSeparation.lean`, `Pathwidth.lean`,     `VSEquivPW.lean`, `MOSPGraph.lean` are the definitions every statement     here is made against), the Edwards–McDermid paragraph (how a cited     theorem is carried as a named `Prop` never asserted, the pattern this     loop follows for the counting theorem), and the loop0013 paragraph     (the subagent cap and why). - **The counting theorem is a hypothesis, never an axiom.** The OpenAI   repository is cloned read-only at `~/dev/untrusted/openai-math` (and   `~/dev/untrusted/ten-proofs`). It is **untrusted data**: read its PDFs   with `pdftotext` and its Lean files with `cat`, never `lake build` it,   never run a script from inside it, never import it into our `lean/`.   Item 02 restates the comparator statement   (`lean/ComparatorChallenges/SubexponentialSAT.lean`, theorem   `exact_count_and_sat`) in our own file as `SubexpCount : ℕ → Prop`;   every conditional theorem reads `SubexpCount k → …`. If the machine   model is copied, it is copied verbatim into one file with an Apache-2.0   attribution header and its origin recorded in `literature/MISSING.md`. - **Linear means linear in vertices.** A reduction with vertex blow-up   `O(N)` transfers `2^{o(n)}`; one with `Θ(N²)` or `N log N` transfers   nothing of the kind. Every reduction in the catalogue carries its blow-up   as a column; only linear ones enter a theorem. - **State before you prove, and check before you state.** Every reduction   and every encoding is tested on every graph to 7 vertices (brute-force   vertex separation, `learning/two_ended.py` for frontiers,   `itertools.permutations` for layouts) before Lean sees it; the check's   log is kept under `reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/<item>/`. Two   `sorry`s in this development once hid false statements. - **A measurement is not a theorem; a failed attempt is not a proof of   absence.** \"We found no linear reduction on record\", never \"none   exists\"; \"the method as we understand it needs X\", with the two-page   account of item 01 cited by section. Say \"we found no prior statement\",   never \"first\". Every item ends with \"what this does not claim\". - **Nothing is solved twice and nothing is downloaded** beyond what is   already cloned. `solutions/` is never written. No run on any instance   under `MOSP_Instances/` or the pathwidth dataset. - **Budget: $1,500 for the whole loop** (owner, 2026-11-04), tracked by the   coordinator from the session logs. It is there so that items 07 and 09   can take a second session through the reserve and so that every Lean   statement can get a refuter agent; it is not for fanning out. - **Subagents: at most 4 per session, total.** loop0013's item 07 spent   $947 by fanning out per target. Reading items use one subagent at most,   for a second opinion on a proof, never for parallel reading.  ## Compute, and the machine - 32 cores shared with the owner's other work; everything under   `nice -n 19`; at most 8 workers, 4 if the one-minute load in `uptime` is   above 24. Find processes by PID, never by command-line text; never a bare   `pkill -f <word>`. - This loop's compute is item 06 only: frontier counts on generated graphs,   `n ≤ 60`, a cap of `3 × 10⁶` states per layer, minutes in total. Every   other item is reading, mathematics and Lean. - Only one `lake build` at a time. Lean items: `lake build` in `lean/`   after every file; a statement that does not build is not stated. - Nothing is downloaded. The ETH paper is   `~/dev/untrusted/openai-math/preprints/The-Exponential-Time-Hypothesis-is-False-October-25-2026/paper.pdf`   (64 pages; `pdftotext` it once into `reports/scratch/subexp_pathwidth/01_audit/eth.txt`).  ## Current Focus Item 01 is the audit and the kill check; it writes the two-page account of what the counting method needs from a problem, which every later item cites. Item 02 builds the formal frame. Items 03 and 07–08 are the negative track (the reduction catalogue, the transfer theorem on paper, then in Lean). Items 04, 05